db/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308185 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308185 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186305285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308185 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d571594f-2518-5623-8366-f5312bc34af9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308207 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "da793013-b50c-5167-aa63-ac5232b9667e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308300 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308300 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308300 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308300 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186307324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308300 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5cf4951-3613-5027-8a9a-0372890bd5d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333213" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) &=-x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t) &=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below regarding the given decoupled system of linear differential equations.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $x$-axis is a {{field-1}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe $y$-axis is a {{field-2}} line.\n\n$\\,$\nThe equilibrium point (origin) is {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14229489-2d69-5bd0-a160-7cf2fe7048a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308409 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308429 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308429 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308429 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308429 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3x-{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nUse the implicit Euler method with one step and Newton's method to approximate $y(0.4).$ Round your answer to two decimal places.\n\n$y(0.4)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b47cc8de-c816-506f-bb22-03bfcc2cbb51" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308429 #edb/ref 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,{y}_{0}=y({x}_{0}),\n$$\n\nthe implicit Euler method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\nΔy={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwith ${y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{\\text{new}})$ evaluated at the next $x$ and $y$ values.\n\nSince we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=0.4,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=0.4-0=0.4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with the implicit Euler method. In general, $Δy$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}_{\\text{new}}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(3({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2})\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute this into ${y}_{\\text{new}}=y+Δy$ and move all terms to the left-hand side and define the resulting expression as $g({y}_{\\text{new}}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})-0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}})=0.\n$$\n\nSo, the general form of the function $g$ for step size $Δx=0.4$ is\n\n$$\ng({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-y-1.2({x}_{\\text{new}}).\n$$\n\nNow, in the first step, the new $x$ value is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx=0+0.4=0.4.$ Then, the function $g$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({y}_{\\text{new}}) & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1-1.2(0.4) \\\\  & =0.4({y}_{\\text{new}})^{2}+{y}_{\\text{new}}-1.48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use a root-finding method with starting point $y=1$ to solve $g({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.$ We'll use Newton's method. \n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{g({y}_{\\text{new}})}{{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})}\n$$\n\nNote that the derivative of $g$ is\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}({y}_{\\text{new}})=0.8({y}_{\\text{new}})+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton's method iterative scheme is as follows:\n\n$$\n({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n+1}=({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-\\frac{0.4(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})^{2}+({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n}-1.48}{0.8(({y}_{\\text{new}})_{n})+1}\n$$\n\nApplying several iterations of Newton's method starting with $y(0)=1,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}_{\\text{new}})_{1} & =1 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{2} & =1.044444 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{3} & ≈1.044014 \\\\ ({y}_{\\text{new}})_{4} & ≈1.044014\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(0.4)≈1.04,$ rounded to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x-2y-3,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nTo approximate the solution, one step of the Runge-Kutta method has already been computed, resulting in the following estimate:\n\n$$\ny(1)≈0\n$$\n\nUse the $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton Method with one step to approximate $y(2).$\n\n*Hint: The corrector equation of the two-step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton method is defined as*\n\n$$\nΔy=\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\n*where ${y}_{p}$ denotes the predictor at the current step, and ${y}_{-1}^{′}$ is the estimated slope at the previous point.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "862cb215-e1c8-5e4c-a0a2-cda6cb44f797" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308488 #edb/ref 1074 "For an initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y(a)=c,\n$$\n\nthe $2$-Step Adams-Bashforth-Moulton (ABM $2$-step) Method with step size $Δx$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Predictor} & : & Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx, \\\\ \\text{Corrector} & : & Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- ${y}^{′}=f(x,y)$ is the estimated slope at the current point $(x,y),$\n\n- ${y}_{-1}^{′}=f({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})$ is the estimated slope at the previous point (${x}_{-1},{y}_{-1}),$ and\n\n- ${y}_{p}^{′}=f({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})$ is the estimated slope at the predicted new point $({x}_{\\text{new}},{y}_{p})=(x+Δx,y+Δ{y}_{p}).$\n\nBefore we start, let's add the initial condition data and Runge-Kutta step data to a table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ |  | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, since we want to find the value of $y$ at $x=2,$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\nΔx=2-1=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the values of ${y}^{′}$ at each point according to the given rule:\n\n- The slope at the previous point $({x}_{-1},{y}_{-1})=(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{-1}^{′} & =2{x}_{-1}-2{y}_{-1}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The slope at the current point $(x,y)=(1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2x-2y-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 0-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ |  |  |  |  |\n\nNext, we compute $Δ{y}_{p}$ using the predictor equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ{y}_{p} & =\\frac{1}{2}(3{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ |  |  |  |\n\nSo, the coordinates of the predicted new point are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2, \\\\ {y}_{p} & =y+Δ{y}_{p}=0+1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's add the second value to the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ |  |  |\n\nHence, the slope at this predicted point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =2{x}_{\\text{new}}-2{y}_{p}-3 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2-2\\cdot 1-3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |  |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using the corrector equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{12}(5{y}_{p}^{′}+8{y}^{′}-{y}_{-1}^{′})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}(5\\cdot (-1)+8\\cdot (-1)-(-5))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot (-8) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe value of $x$ in the next row is ${x}_{\\text{new}}=2.$ To get the value of $y$ in the next row, we add $Δy$ to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =0+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these results to our table gives the following:\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $Δ{y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}$ | ${y}_{p}^{′}$ | $Δy$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $1$ | $-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| $1$ | $0$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{2}{3}$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈-\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308207 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333880" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y=0,\\,y(1)=-1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's Method with two steps to approximate $y(2)$ and ${y}^{′}(2).$\n\n$$\ny(2)≈{{field-1}},\\,{y}^{′}(2)≈{{field-2}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b1c05ae-5800-59fe-b52e-f6754d3810af" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308522 #edb/ref 1074 "For a second-order initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}=f(x,y,{y}^{′}),\\,y(a)=c,\\,{y}^{′}(a)=d,\n$$\n\nwe can use Euler's method to approximate a solution by first reducing the second-order ODE to a system of first-order ODEs,\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(a)=c, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=f(x,y,z)\\, && z(a)=d,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nbefore applying the update rule component-wise to each equation.\n\nTo apply Euler’s method, we first isolate ${y}^{″}$ so it can serve as the derivative of a new variable $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-y & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}{y}^{″} & =y-4{y}^{′} \\\\ {y}^{″} & =\\frac{y-4{y}^{′}}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute our new variable $z={y}^{′},{z}^{′}={y}^{″}$ in the right-hand side of the above equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{z}^{′}=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNote that the initial value of $z$ is $z(1)={y}^{′}(1)=2.$\nSo, the initial value problem reduces to the following system of first-order ODEs:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} &=z,\\, && y(1)=-1, \\\\ {z}^{′} &=\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}},\\, && z(1)=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed with Euler's method by applying the update rule component-wise.\n\nFirst, note that we are given the values of $y$ and $z={y}^{′}$ at $x=1$ and asked to find their values at $x=2.$ The distance from $x=1$ to $x=2$ is $1,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(1)=-1$ and $z(1)=2.$\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ |  |  | $2$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 1**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the first row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=2 \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{-1-4\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}}=-9 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=1 \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-9\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{9}{2} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=-1+1=0 \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=2-\\frac{9}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ |  |  |\n\n**Step 2**.\n\nWe compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy,$ ${z}^{′}$ and $Δz$ (using the values of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ in the second row), and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}},$ ${y}_{\\text{new}},$ and ${z}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =z=-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ {z}^{′} & =\\frac{y-4z}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{0-4(-\\frac{5}{2})}{{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}=\\frac{10}{(\\frac{9}{4})}=\\frac{40}{9} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ Δz & ={z}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{40}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{20}{9} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=0-\\frac{5}{4}=-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {z}_{\\text{new}} & =z+Δz=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{20}{9}=-\\frac{5}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add these values to our table.\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | ${y}^{′}$ | $Δy$ | $z$ | ${z}^{′}$ | $Δz$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $2$ | $-9$ | $-\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $0$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ | $-\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\frac{40}{9}$ | $\\frac{20}{9}$ |\n| $2$ | $-\\frac{5}{4}$ |  |  | $-\\frac{5}{18}$ |  |  |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n$y(2)≈-\\frac{5}{4}$\nand\n${y}^{′}(2)=z(2)≈-\\frac{5}{18}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d942a907-7558-5547-92ea-8c390f2de7e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308581 #edb/ref 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4+\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{(-4+\\text{i})t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ {e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}{e}^{-4t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial condition $x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}+3\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+7\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{-4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334866" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1071 "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $3.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.3$ produces a local truncation error (LTE) of $τ(0.3)≈0.162.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.1,$ what LTE should we expect?\n\n$τ(0.1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce506036-afe4-59f4-88ca-ada7f672db57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308699 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.3$ to $0.1.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.3}{0.1}=3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $3,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{3+1}={3}^{4}=81.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.1,$ the expected LTE is\n\n$$\nτ(0.1)≈\\frac{0.162}{81}=0.002.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-335106" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nGiven the matrices $A$ and ${e}^{At}$ above, compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}).$\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8aaeaee6-5815-57be-985f-e23b60c35867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308728 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At})=A\\,{e}^{At}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{At}) & =A\\,{e}^{At} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 